Agent Prayers


Introduction

One of the characteristic features of Ouroborus is that agents living on the 
cellular automaton grid can be thought of as a number of things, depending on 
one's focus. Thus agents are:

1) a string containing a sequence of letter groupings codifying genetic 
information, i.e. a genome.
2) a script in Pyrex with some executable code written from the genome. Python 
imports this into the runtime envirnoment, i.e. a module.
3) a dictionary-type object, with various attributes such as name, genome, 
module, grid address and so on, i.e. a bookentry in a list which is an attribute
of GOD.Organizer
4) a birdcage agent object which is called by GOD.Organizer through the 
bookentry.

It is of course naturally simple for GOD.Organizer to send information to the 
agents, via the respective bookentries which are callable subobjects of a simple
list attribute. GOD.Organizer can retrieve information from the agents with the 
same ease, when this is part of the silicon demiurge's execution loop. However 
some more elaborate means of data transmission is required for the agents 
themselves to send information to GOD.Organizer, so to speak out of their own 
volition. It is exactly for this purpose that agent prayers were introduced.


Technical Details

The GOD module consists of three main objects, Generator, Organizer and 
Destroyer. The Generator is in charge of initialising everything and the 
Destroyer's role is left as an exercise to the reader. (A very trivial one.) It 
is the Organizer which concerns us here. 

All the prayers have corresponding methods in the Organizer. Their names are 
prefixed by the string "grantPrayer" followed by the prayer name. This methods 
are called through reflection (using the getattr function) by the readBookOfLife
method of the Organizer. It is important to notice that the prayer method in 
question is being called and returned simultaneously by the readBookofLife 
method's return statement.

So far, the following prayers are implemented:

1) CreateMe
This prayer should be set when an agent is first created. It populates the new 
agent's bookentry. In doing that it sets its prayer to BeBirthed. (NOTE: It also
does some sound related initialisation which also needs to be explained.)

2) BeBirthed
This method takes the newly created agent and makes it live, that is, it
registers the agent with the relevant automaton instance, as well as calling
any other method needed to initialise it. Last, but not least, it sets the 
prayer to Live.

3) Live
This is the default prayer, it executes the main loop of the agent, so to speak.
(The agents don't actually have a main loop, but more on this later.)

4) GrantChild
This prayer is a request for reproduction. It resets the parent's prayer to 
Live. (NOTE: Oddly enough, it sets the childs prayer to BeBirthed, thus 
contradicting this text, as well as the systems architechture as this author, 
Jergas, understands it. MEA CULPA: Methinks this is my fault, as I remeber 
having hacked this aspect of the prayer system before properly understanding 
it.)

5) KillMe
This prayer is a destructor method, so to speak, it should contain everything 
needed to properly erase (or kill) an agent. For obvious reasons, this is the
only prayer, apart from Live, that doesn't need to reset the calling agent's 
prayer.
